From 278e701970a07f881fefbfb68a640643bf90af14 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nuk3s <44327449+nuk3s@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 20:42:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kv-offload): pad disk slots to 4096 bytes under O_DIRECT

Upstream base: the patch applies to local-inference-lab/vllm at
19f2c20ed4d64ae22bd009ceacadd85e2a81e354 after the 702 patches of
spark-ds41f-1120 (candidate-62, 0001 to 0829).

Rationale: DiskBackend.init asserted that total_block_bytes is a
multiple of 4096 under O_DIRECT. The packed block of DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash
is 227,840 bytes, which leaves a remainder of 2,560, so the disk tier
could not start unless it used the page cache, and the page cache takes
the host memory that the tier is meant to save. Removing the assert is
not enough, because slot n of an unpadded staging buffer starts at n *
227,840, and a drive with 4096-byte logical blocks rejects such offsets,
lengths, and addresses with EINVAL. This patch therefore stores each
block in a slot of disk_slot_bytes(block) bytes, which is the block
rounded up to a multiple of 4096 under O_DIRECT and the exact block size
with use_page_cache. Each staging slot is one contiguous, 4096-aligned
row that holds the tensors' bytes back to back and then zero padding,
and each slot has its own DMA parameters, which address it as block 0,
and moves to or from the file in one iovec. The worker and the scheduler
both count the slots as capacity // disk_slot_bytes(block), and the
connector passes use_page_cache to the scheduler for that count, so the
scheduler never hands out a slot past the end of the file.

Quality implications: the patch does not change outputs. A slot in the
file differs from the old layout only by the padding at its end, which
costs 1,536 bytes of disk for each block (0.67%) and keeps every offset,
length, and address aligned to 4096 bytes on either drive format.

Tests: tests/v1/simple_kv_offload/test_ds41_offload.py writes
227,840-byte blocks to slots 0, 31, and 63 of a real O_DIRECT file
through the real staging and pwritev/preadv code, with the CUDA batch
copy replaced by a host memmove, then reads them back into a cleared
pool and compares them byte for byte. The test runs with 2 and with 4
staging slots, both directly and through the IO threads, and it checks
that the file holds each block at slot * 229,376 followed by zero
padding. Further tests check that two tensors of 1,536 and 2,048 bytes
share one 4,096-byte slot in tensor order, and that the scheduler and
the worker count the same slots at 100, 200, and 400 GiB. The disk of
the test machine has 512-byte logical sectors, so the EINVAL case of a
4096-byte drive was not reproduced. The alignment case of test_worker.py,
which needs CUDA, now expects the padded size, and test_kv_events.py
sizes its disk tiers in padded slots. On the CPU path the fork's offload
tests passed 88 before this patch and 101 with the new tests, with 2
CUDA-only modules skipped. On DGX Spark hardware the patch ran only as
part of the whole series, which the experiment's README describes.

Upstream status: the patch is proposed to spark-ds41f in
experiments/2026-11-22-kv-disk-tier and has not been submitted to
local-inference-lab/vllm.
